# PWA App Icon Style

When creating or regenerating PWA icons, use this style:

## Source

`logo/light-bg/chathub-pwa-icon-light-bg-transparent-outside.png` (1024x1024)

## Style

- **Rounded rectangle** with ~22% corner radius
- **Background inside rounded area**: light blue `#cde2fa` (rgb 205,226,250)
- **Corners outside rounded area**: fully transparent
- **No partial transparency** at edges — clean cut between opaque center and transparent corners

## Critical: `any` vs `maskable` — DIFFERENT strategies

This is the most important rule. **Never use the same image for both purposes.**

| Purpose | Platform usage | Icon strategy |
|---------|---------------|---------------|
| `any` | **Windows** (taskbar, start menu), macOS older, favicon contexts | Rounded rectangle with **transparent corners** |
| `maskable` | Android, iOS, macOS Sonoma+, Chrome OS | **Full-bleed opaque** — OS applies its own mask shape |

**Why:** Maskable icons with transparent corners get filled by the OS (white on Android, background_color on others). Windows uses `any` icons and natively supports transparency.

Sources: [StackOverflow](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/72504764), [Progressier](https://progressier.com/maskable-icons-editor), [dev.to](https://dev.to/progressier/why-a-pwa-app-icon-shouldnt-have-a-purpose-set-to-any-maskable-4c78), [Logo Foundry](https://logofoundry.app/blog/pwa-icon-requirements-safe-areas)

## Manifest configuration

- **`background_color`**: REMOVE (do not set). Windows uses this to fill transparent icon areas. Removing it lets native transparency work.
- **`theme_color`**: `#cde2fa` — for the title bar / browser chrome in standalone mode
- **`_getImage`**: MUST use the actual icon `url` parameter, NOT override with `BRANDING_LOGO_URL`. The old code `BRANDING_LOGO_URL || url` broke all icons by serving the transparent branding logo instead.

